<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corinth Canal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a>–<a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Corinth Canal is a canal across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, connecting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saronic-gulf">Saronic Gulf</a> and separating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> from the mainland. Projects to cut it date back to antiquity, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>'s attempt in 67 CE. Built between 1881 and 1893, it is about 6.4 kilometres long but only around 21 metres wide, too narrow for most modern ships.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corinth-canal.jpg" alt="Corinth Canal" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Winston Cooke · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corinth_Canal_in_Greece.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
